## Summary

Renamed every snake_case-with-`_t`-suffix type to PascalCase to match Rust naming convention (`ptls_cipher_suite_t` → `PtlsCipherSuite`, `ptls_t` → `Ptls`, `cipher_suites_t` → `CipherSuiteEntry`, `tls_ctx_t` → `TlsCtx`, etc.), which let me drop the module-level `#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]` lint allowance. Switched all 14 callback traits from `&mut self` to `&self` — none of the C typedefs carry a `void* ctx`, so the implementor has no required state, and this lets the registry accessors hand out plain `Option<&'static dyn Trait>` instead of the `Option<&'static mut dyn Trait>` aliasing smell. Dropped the `_fn` C-derived suffix from the `register_*` setter functions and from the corresponding registry accessor functions, and renamed the C-flavored `free_certificate_verifier_fn` field on `CertificateVerifier` to `dispose_verifier`. Tightened the docstrings: each public item now leads with what the Rust API does and ends with `C: \`name\`` for traceability rather than the inverted form. Updated the only sibling consumer (`tls_api.rs`) to import the renamed `PtlsCipherSuite`.


